WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.320
It's thrilling to see someone&nbsp;
who we've only see under a mask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.840
reveal their face. But what are the very&nbsp;
best face reveals in TV history? Does the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.920
unmasking of one especially fast superhero&nbsp;
make the list? Keep watching to find out.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:20.400
One of the most immediately obvious&nbsp;
things that sets "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.400
apart from most other live-action TV shows is&nbsp;
the relative scarcity of human faces in it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.000
Sure, there are some bare-faced&nbsp;
folks in the supporting cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.840
but our two leads are an alien puppet that&nbsp;
doesn't speak and a mostly silent hero who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:42.960
never removes his helmet. That is, until the first&nbsp;
season finale, titled "Chapter 8: Redemption."

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.560
Our hero, Din Djarin, is a member of a&nbsp;
highly orthodox branch of Mandalorians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:53.360
called the Children of the Watch. One of the&nbsp;
rules of this order is that its members are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.800
never permitted to show their faces to any other&nbsp;
living being. And throughout the first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.480
Din abides by this rule at all times, so much&nbsp;
so that not even the audience gets a chance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.920
see his face. However, during a climactic&nbsp;
battle with the forces of the evil Moff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.360
Gideon, Din is gravely injured and finds&nbsp;
himself in dire need of medical attention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:22.000
His droid companion, IG-11, leans in to remove&nbsp;
Din's helmet so that he can better administer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.640
treatment. But Din draws his blaster&nbsp;
and points it at the droid, saying,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.360
"Try it, and I'll kill you. It is forbidden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.000
No living thing has seen me without&nbsp;
my helmet since I swore the creed.”

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.360
"I am not a living thing."

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:43.120
IG-11 removes it anyway. Then, for the first time&nbsp;
in the series, audiences got a chance to look into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:50.480
the big sad puppy dog eyes of actor Pedro Pascal.&nbsp;
At that moment, the Mandalorian became a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:56.720
less mysterious and a little more human — a trend&nbsp;
that would continue into the show's 2nd season.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.200
Throughout most of the run of&nbsp;
"Avatar: The Last Airbender,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.240
Aang the Avatar is being hunted by&nbsp;
a disgraced Fire Nation prince named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:13.200
Zuko. They wish to capture the Avatar in order&nbsp;
to restore Zuko’s honor. However, in Season 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:19.600
Episode 13, a different servant of the Fire&nbsp;
Nation beats Zuko to it when Aang is captured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.960
by the forces of Admiral Zhao and locked&nbsp;
up in the sovereign state's military base.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.800
Aang finds himself surrounded by guards with&nbsp;
his arms and legs in irons to prevent him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.240
from using his bending abilities. For&nbsp;
a time, all hope seems lost. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:41.120
a mysterious silent figure in a blue&nbsp;
demon mask arrives on the scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.200
This stranger frees Aang from his&nbsp;
bonds and helps him escape the base.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.840
“Who are you? What’s going on?&nbsp;
Are you here to rescue me?”

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.640
However, as the two are on the verge of escaping&nbsp;
into the woods, Aang's rescuer is struck in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.160
face by an arrow. His mask takes most of&nbsp;
the blow, but he is knocked unconscious.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.400
Aang kicks up some dust and unmasks&nbsp;
him, revealing the face of Prince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.400
Zuko. Aang considers leaving Zuko for the&nbsp;
guards, but takes him to safety instead. Aang&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.680
waits alongside the unconscious prince, hoping&nbsp;
to talk with him when he wakes up, but as soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:23.760
as Zuko regains his facilities, he attacks&nbsp;
Aang, forcing the Avatar to flee once again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.560
The prince never gives Aang an explanation&nbsp;
for why he rescued him, but this moment ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.080
up being the first sign that there's much more&nbsp;
going on with Zuko than Aang initially believed.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:40.240
"The Twilight Zone" is a show that's famous&nbsp;
for ending its stories with a memorable twist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.880
and perhaps no twist is more memorable than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.960
the one that comes at the climax of&nbsp;
the episode "Eye of the Beholder."

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:53.040
The protagonist is a woman named Janet Tyler.&nbsp;
She lies in a hospital bed, her face concealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:59.280
by bandages. Various doctors and nurses are&nbsp;
hanging around as well, but their faces are always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.960
off-screen or concealed in shadow. The doctors&nbsp;
say that Janet is disfigured, and although she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.880
has undergone a series of treatments in order to&nbsp;
correct her appearance, thus far, the treatments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.400
have been unsuccessful. Additionally, Janet's&nbsp;
now reached the maximum number of times she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.760
allowed to attempt the procedure by law. This&nbsp;
means that if it doesn't work this time, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.360
won't be allowed to try again, and she'll have to&nbsp;
live with her appearance for the rest of her life.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:31.760
At the episode's climax, when&nbsp;
Janet's bandages are finally removed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.280
she looks totally fine. However, the doctors&nbsp;
are horrified, disappointed that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:43.040
still been no change. Their faces are now&nbsp;
also finally revealed for the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:50.320
and we see large nostrils, sunken eyes, and a&nbsp;
variety of swollen, drooping, or asymmetrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.840
features. Sure, maybe the metaphor seems a&nbsp;
bit obvious or heavy-handed, but "Eye of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.840
Beholder" is still an all-time classic episode of&nbsp;
television. Even knowing the twist ahead of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:06.600
we dare to give it a watch and not let&nbsp;
out a bit of a gasp when that reveal hits.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:13.840
"Death Note" tells the story of a supernatural&nbsp;
serial killer named Light Yagami and the various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.120
law enforcement officials that try — and usually&nbsp;
fail — to catch him. The only person who ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:25.040
up being a true equal to Light is a mysterious&nbsp;
independent detective who goes by the pseudonym&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.320
"L." And even though L is widely regarded&nbsp;
as the greatest detective in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.640
no one knows his true name, and&nbsp;
no one has ever seen his face.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.200
For a time, L only works with&nbsp;
Japanese law enforcement remotely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.800
communicating over voice chat. But eventually,&nbsp;
the police begin to distrust L and consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:51.840
cutting ties with him completely. So, as a show of&nbsp;
good faith, L finally shows his face in Season 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:57.840
Episode 6, "Unraveling." He reluctantly agrees&nbsp;
to meet with the team of officers assigned to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.760
serial killer case in person in a hotel room.&nbsp;
When the officers enter the room and see L,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:10.560
the camera slowly pans up L's body, revealing a&nbsp;
skinny teenager. His posture is terrible, he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:16.800
bags under his eyes, his hair is long and unkempt,&nbsp;
and his clothes are way too big for him. Needless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.760
to say, this is not what any of them imagined&nbsp;
the world's greatest detective would look like.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:28.560
One of the most enduring versions of the surprise&nbsp;
unmasking trope is the version that also comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.160
with a surprise gender reveal. The best execution&nbsp;
of this version of the trope in recent memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.520
was in the "Game of Thrones" episode "What&nbsp;
Is Dead May Never Die." In this episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:45.120
Catelyn Stark journeys to the camp of Renly&nbsp;
Baratheon, seeking to make an alliance with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.760
the young lord. However, she arrives in&nbsp;
the middle of a tournament when Renly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:55.280
is watching a battle between two figures&nbsp;
in armor. The first is Ser Loras Tyrell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:59.920
one of the most skilled knights in&nbsp;
Westeros and Renly's brother-in-law.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:06.080
The second is a character we've never seen&nbsp;
before — a towering stranger in copper armor.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.680
Despite being completely unknown to the crowd&nbsp;
and not even a knight, this strapping newcomer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:17.120
defeats Loras swiftly and definitively.&nbsp;
After kneeling before the Lord Renly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:23.440
the stranger then removes their helmet, revealing&nbsp;
the face of a woman. She is Brienne of Tarth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:29.200
a lady who is of noble birth and twice the warrior&nbsp;
of any man she's ever met, but because of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.480
gender, she is not allowed to become a knight.&nbsp;
Nonetheless, because of her impressive display&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:41.040
of martial prowess, Renly agrees to break with&nbsp;
tradition and accept Brienne into his Kingsguard.

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:41.540
"Done!"

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.520
It's one hell of a character&nbsp;
introduction, and from here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.720
Brienne of Tarth goes on to become one of the&nbsp;
most compelling characters in the entire series.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.480
There are several times throughout the series&nbsp;
"Squid Game" when mysterious characters are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:02.880
unmasked and secret identities are revealed. But&nbsp;
strangely enough, the most emotionally impactful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.640
unmasking in the series occurs not with a major&nbsp;
character but with someone who doesn't even get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:14.720
name. In the episode "The Man With the Umbrella,"&nbsp;
players have to cleanly cut a small shape out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:21.120
a larger piece of Dalgona, a Korean honeycomb&nbsp;
candy. If they break the shape while doing so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.000
they will be executed by one of the many&nbsp;
armed guards standing around the room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.760
Toward the end of the game, one of the players,&nbsp;
Player 119, breaks the shape he's trying to cut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:38.080
He is about to be executed by a nearby guard when&nbsp;
at the last moment, he steals the guard's gun.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.760
As a dozen or so armed guards close in around&nbsp;
him, Player 119 ["one nineteen"] forces one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:50.320
the high-ranking employees in square masks — the&nbsp;
ones known as "managers" — to unmask himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:55.920
And when this manager takes off his mask, he&nbsp;
reveals the face of an ordinary young man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:01.600
This reveal finally solidifies to the participants&nbsp;
that whatever is going on with this game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:08.400
isn't supernatural or fantastical. These guards&nbsp;
aren't aliens or robots — these are just humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:15.760
engaging in an all-too-human form of evil. Before&nbsp;
turning the gun on himself, Player 119 comments,

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:18.640
"You're so young. How did you end up like this?"

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:24.320
Moments later, the mysterious Front Man&nbsp;
arrives on the scene and, without ceremony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:29.520
shoots the unmasked manager in the head. He&nbsp;
then tells his assembled masked employees,

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:32.920
"Remember. Once they find out&nbsp;
who you are, you're dead."

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:40.320
In Season 3, Episode 2 of "Rick and Morty" — an&nbsp;
episode called "Rickmancing the Stone" — Rick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:46.000
Morty, and Summer find themselves in dire&nbsp;
need of a vacation. Using Rick's portal gun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:52.400
the trio ends up in a post-apocalyptic version&nbsp;
of Earth, a Mad Max-esque world of murderous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:59.040
warlords driving around in rusty hot rods. Hoping&nbsp;
to put off returning to Earth as long as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:04.960
Summer ends up joining a band of warriors known&nbsp;
as the Death Stalkers, led by a muscle-bound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:10.800
warrior named "Hemorrhage", whose face is&nbsp;
concealed by a helmet shaped like a bucket.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:16.640
Later, after going on a raid together, Summer&nbsp;
and Hemorrhage have an intimate conversation in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:21.840
the Death Stalker's garage, during which&nbsp;
Summer asks him if she can see his face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.880
In his low, gravelly voice, Hemorrhage replies,

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.840
"No one has seen my true face and lived."

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.160
But then, he relents, removing his&nbsp;
helmet. Summer is then shocked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:37.040
find that Hemorrhage isn't at all what she&nbsp;
expected. He isn't horribly disfigured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:42.800
but he isn't roguishly handsome either. Instead,&nbsp;
he's just kinda dorky looking, with a head that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:49.440
is far paler than the rest of his deeply tanned&nbsp;
body, a bad haircut, and a terrible mustache.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:53.760
This unexpected reveal ends up being one&nbsp;
of the funniest moments of the series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:58.200
and completely unlike any other&nbsp;
face reveal in TV history.

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:05.280
Early on in the episode of "Firefly" titled&nbsp;
"Our Mrs. Reynolds", Captain Malcolm Reynolds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:09.840
and the rest of his crew indulge in a long&nbsp;
night of drinking and merriment with some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:15.280
friendly strangers. This is on a small planet&nbsp;
in the middle of nowhere. The next morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:20.480
Mal discovers that because he drunkenly&nbsp;
participated in some sort of local ritual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:25.040
that he didn't understand, he is&nbsp;
now married to a woman he just met.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:25.600
“Who are you?”

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.480
“Mr. Reynolds, sir, I’m your wife.”

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:36.720
She’s a meek and kindhearted woman named&nbsp;
Saffron. However, by the end of the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:41.680
Saffron reveals that she is actually a&nbsp;
skilled con artist who is only tagging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.680 --> 00:11:46.880
along with Mal and his crew so that she can rob&nbsp;
them of everything they're worth. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:53.920
our heroes manage to stop Saffron's plan, leaving&nbsp;
her behind on a snowy planet to an uncertain fate.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:59.120
Five episodes later, in an episode titled&nbsp;
"Trash," Malcolm meets up with an old war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:05.280
buddy named Monty. During their conversation,&nbsp;
Monty tells Mal that he recently got married,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:10.480
and he can't wait to introduce Mal to&nbsp;
his new bride, a woman named Bridget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:16.720
When Bridget is finally introduced to Mal, she&nbsp;
has an all-too-familiar face. That's right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.440
Monty has also fallen prey to the&nbsp;
con artist that Mal knew as Saffron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:26.560
and her second appearance ends up being an&nbsp;
even wilder ride than the first time around.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.400
The threat of superheroes being unmasked&nbsp;
by villains is a long-standing trope in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:37.920
superhero fiction. Generally, this idea is&nbsp;
played pretty straight. It's assumed that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:43.680
a villain ever unmasks a hero, they can learn&nbsp;
that hero's secret identity and threaten them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:49.120
and their loved ones. However, there is a&nbsp;
particularly hilarious inversion of this trope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:55.120
that occurs in an episode of "Justice League&nbsp;
Unlimited" titled "The Great Brain Robbery."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.120 --> 00:13:01.920
After the evil Lex Luthor's latest invention — a&nbsp;
mind-reading machine — unexpectedly malfunctions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:05.280
Luthor ends up swapping minds with The Flash.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:11.200
After Luthor in the Flash's body is chased around&nbsp;
the League's headquarters by the team, he finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:17.120
manages to escape and has a moment to himself in&nbsp;
one of the base's bathrooms. Looking at himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:23.360
in the mirror in the Flash's body and wearing the&nbsp;
Flash's superhero costume, Luthor says to himself,

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:26.880
"If nothing else, I can at least&nbsp;
learn the Flash's secret identity."

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:35.040
He then unmasks himself and looks in the mirror at&nbsp;
Wally West's unmasked face. After a beat, he says,

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:37.680
"I have no idea who this is."

